# shell: desktop
# home: fixture
# prefs: factory
# C carries a remark from one line to the next, and this is the claim a
# line-local classifier could not make.
#
# The file holds the SAME words twice -- `int x = 42;` -- once inside a
# block remark opened on an earlier line and once outside it.  Nothing
# on either line says which is which; only what came before does.  The
# two pixels below are the SAME glyph of the same word, two rows apart,
# and they are asserted in both colours: what is proven is that the
# runtime tells them apart, not merely that something was coloured.
#
# The pixels were probed rather than guessed, and they are GLYPH pixels
# -- the ink a letter is drawn with, not chrome.
wait 25
click 980 30
wait 4
menu-pick Applications
wait 4
menu-pick Editor
wait 90
expect-title Edit
click 60 8
wait 6
menu-pick Open…
wait 40
expect-title Open
click 400 200
wait 6
click 675 413
wait 60
expect-no-title Open
click-window Edit
wait 25
shot /tmp/tiku-editor-ccolour.png
note inside the remark: the words are what the machine ignores, however
note much they look like a language
expect-pixel 275 167 607060
note and outside it, two lines down, the very same word is a word the
note language reserves
expect-pixel 275 205 0000b0
note neither is the other, which is the whole of what carrying means
expect-pixel 275 167 607060
note put the editor away rather than leave it standing
key Escape
wait 30
expect-no-title Edit
